title: Passwords are gone. It’s just you and your device.
type: update
date: 2026-06-03
createdAt: 2026-06-03T21:30:00Z
releaseAt: 2026-06-03T22:00:00Z
author: Richard D.
stream: strategy
tags: passkey, security, simplicity, ux

# The Change
body[1]{content}:
  "At Pharos, we want you to spend your time designing kitchens, not managing a list of passwords. That’s why we’ve made a big change: we’ve completely removed passwords from Pharos Kitchen Design. We aren't just 'preferring' passkeys; we’ve completely deleted the old way of doing things. No more 'forgot password' emails, and no more shared secrets. It’s just you and your device."

# What this means for you
details[3]{focus, description}:
  Nothing to remember, "You’ll never have to type a password here again. To log in, you just use what you already have—your phone, your laptop, or a security key. It’s one step and you’re in."
  Nothing to steal, "Since we don’t store passwords, there’s nothing for a hacker to take from us. Your login 'key' never leaves your device. We couldn't give it away even if we wanted to."
  Privacy by default, "We’re not the middleman for your identity. By using a Passkey, we’re making sure that you are the only one who can access your account."

# Why we did it
closing[1]{content}:
  "We realized that 'good enough' security wasn't good enough for you. We wanted a system that was actually secure, not just one that felt familiar. It’s a simpler way to work. We think you’re going to love it."
